# Krux release kernel: no crash-persisted data.
#
# An unlocked bootloader makes the runtime debug surface (dmesg,
# debugfs, kallsyms, SysRq, the serial console) a non-issue: anyone can
# boot their own kernel.  What matters is what this kernel leaves for
# the *next* kernel to read:
#
#   /proc/last_kmsg  <- CONFIG_ANDROID_RAM_CONSOLE
#   pstore/ramoops   <- CONFIG_RAMOOPS (and CONFIG_PSTORE)
#   core files       <- CONFIG_ELF_CORE (the stub also sets RLIMIT_CORE=0)
#
# The reserved 1 MB region behind the ram console stays: the kernel-only
# wipe writes its power-off marker into the spare tail at 0x100F0000
# (the ram console used only the first 256 KB).  With
# CONFIG_ANDROID_PERSISTENT_RAM=y and CONFIG_ANDROID_RAM_CONSOLE=n the
# region is still reserved, but nothing logs into it.
#
# How to apply: edit the build's .config, run `make olddefconfig`, then
# rebuild zImage-dtb.  This fragment is data for that manual step; the
# current build-kernel.sh profile format only knows symbols to turn off,
# so it does not apply this file by itself.
CONFIG_ANDROID_PERSISTENT_RAM=y
# CONFIG_ANDROID_RAM_CONSOLE is not set
# CONFIG_RAMOOPS is not set
# CONFIG_PSTORE is not set
# CONFIG_ELF_CORE is not set
